    {
      "type": "done",
      "answer": "Using Amdahl’s Law for a fixed problem size, the speedup with p processors is:\n\nS(p) = 1 / ((1 - f) + f / p)\n\nGiven f = 0.9:\n\n1) With p = 10 processors:\nS(10) = 1 / ((1 - 0.9) + 0.9 / 10)\n     = 1 / (0.1 + 0.09)\n     = 1 / 0.19\n     ≈ 5.26x\n\n2) With infinitely many processors (p → ∞):\nThe term f / p → 0, so:\nS(∞) = 1 / (1 - f) = 1 / 0.1 = 10x\n\nThis matches the lecture’s numerical intuition that for f = 0.90 the maximum speedup is 10x [S1]. The calculation and the limiting value are both supported by the formula and the example on page 66 [S1].",
      "citations": [
        {
          "document_id": "34911c46e78edc833f283f8dd678b85d6f8c92d66859e6bf6886949181d95402",
          "chunk_id": "34911c46e78edc833f283f8dd678b85d6f8c92d66859e6bf6886949181d95402-p66-1",
          "page": 66,
          "text": "Performance Models\n  Amdahl's Law: The Serial Fraction Wins\n\n\n\n ¡ When problem size is fixed, even a small serial fraction caps total speedup\n\n\n S(p) = 1 / ((1 - f) + f / p)\n                                                          FIXED PROBLEM SIZE\n\n\n                                                                                          serial            parallelizable work f: parallelizable fraction\n1 - f: serial fraction\n p: number of processors / threads                        More processors squeeze the blue part, but\n                                                                     the purple serial part remains.\nNUMERICAL INTUITION\n\n             f = 0.90                                 f = 0.99\n\n    max speedup -> 10x          max speedup -> 100x",
          "preview_url": "/api/documents/34911c46e78edc833f283f8dd678b85d6f8c92d66859e6bf6886949181d95402/pages/66/preview",
          "source": "S1"
